 - NEVER ship `self_update` without its `signatures` feature (zipsign). A checksum from the same release proves nothing.
-- NEVER let `run --mutants` wipe any directory except the Windows host mutation scratch `<repo parent>/viola-mutants-scratch`. A pure guard refuses (`scratch-refused`) unless the resolved path's final component is exactly `viola-mutants-scratch` and the repository is neither that directory nor inside it. A drive or filesystem root has no final component, so it is refused. A failed removal is `scratch-wipe-failed`. NEVER fall back to `%TEMP%` or the repository, and NEVER print the path. The guard and the wipe run only on a Windows host (`HOST_SCRATCH = cfg!(windows)`); elsewhere the harness touches no scratch, and the Linux dev host's same-named NOCOW `TMPDIR` for mutation runs is the operator's, never wiped by the harness.
+- NEVER let `run --mutants` wipe any directory except the Windows host mutation scratch `<repo parent>/viola-mutants-scratch`. A pure guard refuses (`scratch-refused`) unless the resolved path's final component is exactly `viola-mutants-scratch` and the repository is neither that directory nor inside it. A drive or filesystem root has no final component, so it is refused. A failed removal is `scratch-wipe-failed`. The wiped scratch is re-created owner-only through `viola_state::fs::create_private_dir` (the protected user + SYSTEM DACL on Windows), so an instance tree a test lays inside it passes the strict-modes check as one under `%TEMP%` does; NEVER re-create it with a plain `create_dir_all`, whose inherited workspace-drive grants the check refuses, and NEVER loosen the check for it. NEVER fall back to `%TEMP%` or the repository, and NEVER print the path. The guard and the wipe run only on a Windows host (`HOST_SCRATCH = cfg!(windows)`); elsewhere the harness touches no scratch, and the Linux dev host's same-named NOCOW `TMPDIR` for mutation runs is the operator's, never wiped by the harness.
 - NEVER let a test-side removal under `target/e2e-home` take anything but a dir the test side itself created: the root fixture chain's owner sweep and a test home's drop (by `owner.json` record, by its own `TempDir`) and harness `cleanup` (by the `viola-session-` name). On the Linux dev host that path is a link to the per-user tmpfs directory `/tmp/viola-e2e-home-<uid>`, so these creations and removals reach outside the working directory: a boundary widening, ratified by the founder (live, 2026-10-07T07:25Z, after it was shown to him as creating and deleting outside the working directory through the link; relayed by the overseer). The two keepers (`prepare_home_base` in `tests/support/home.rs`, `Workspace::ensure_e2e_home` in the harness) NEVER follow a linked base unless its target is absolute and one `lstat` of it reads a real directory with no group or other bit; a target that is itself a link is refused, and a refusal is a fixed message that names no path. They create the target with mode 0700 through the directory builder (never a chmod after, never a parent) and remove nothing. Ownership is not read separately: the `tempdir_in` that follows succeeds in an owner-only directory only for its owner. `viola` itself never reads or writes the backing by that name, and no CI runner has the link.
